#000000\" face=\"Times New Roman, serif\"><span style=\"font-size:13.3333px\">                                                                  </span></font></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"line-height:18.4px\"><font color=\"#000000\" face=\"Times New Roman, serif\"><span style=\"font-size:13.3333px\">  Robust RMSEA                                               0.077</span></font></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"line-height:18.4px\"><font color=\"#000000\" face=\"Times New Roman, serif\"><span style=\"font-size:13.3333px\">  90 Percent confidence interval - lower                     0.075</span></font></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"line-height:18.4px\"><font color=\"#000000\" face=\"Times New Roman, serif\"><span style=\"font-size:13.3333px\">  90 Percent confidence interval - upper                     0.079</span></font></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"line-height:18.4px\"><font color=\"#000000\" face=\"Times New Roman, serif\"><span style=\"font-size:13.3333px\"><br></span></font></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"line-height:18.4px\"><font color=\"#000000\" face=\"Times New Roman, serif\"><span style=\"font-size:13.3333px\">Standardized Root Mean Square Residual:</span></font></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"line-height:18.4px\"><font color=\"#000000\" face=\"Times New Roman, serif\"><span style=\"line-height:15.3333px;font-size:13.3333px\"></span></font></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"line-height:18.4px\"><font color=\"#000000\" face=\"Times New Roman, serif\"><span style=\"font-size:13.3333px\">  SRMR                                           0.094       0.094</span></font></p></div><div><br></div><div>Now the test statistic is larger than the degrees of freedom, and fit indices make more sense (although the model clearly does not fit). Why is the pooled test statistic much smaller than the test statistic for individual dataset, and why does it turn negative when I try to get the robust test statistic?</div><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0);font-size:medium\"></span></div>"
