   "subject": "Re: Pooled test statistic",
   "parts": [
    [
     2,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"border-left:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div><div><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif;font-size:10pt\">I get the error message: </span></div><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"font-size:12pt;font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif;color:rgb(0,0,0);line-height:18.4px\"><span style=\"font-size:10pt;line-height:15.3333px\"></span></p><div><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin:0in 0in 0.0001pt;font-size:12pt;font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif;color:rgb(0,0,0);line-height:18.4px\"><span style=\"font-size:10pt;line-height:15.3333px\">Negative pooled test statistic was set to zero, so fit will appear to be arbitrarily perfect. Robust corrections uninformative, not returned.</span></p></div></div></div></blockquote><div><br></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div>That is not an error message.  It is just information. </div></div>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><br></div><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"border-left:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"font-size:12pt;font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif;color:rgb(0,0,0);line-height:18.4px\"><span style=\"font-size:10pt;line-height:15.3333px\"></span></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"font-size:12pt;font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif;color:rgb(0,0,0);line-height:18.4px\"><span style=\"color:rgb(34,34,34);font-family:Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;font-size:13px\">Why is the pooled test statistic much smaller than the test statistic for individual dataset, and why does it turn negative when I try to get the robust test statistic?</span></p></div></div></blockquote><div><br></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div>Not sure, but make sure this is still occurring in the latest semTools: </div><div><br></div><div><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\">devtools</span><span style=\"color:#660\">::</span><span style=\"color:#000\">install_github</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#080\">&quot;simsem/semTools/semTools&quot;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span></div></code></div><br></div><div>You can also fit the model to each imputed data set, save the 10 chi-squared stats in a vector, and send it yourself to the<font face=\"courier new, monospace\"> calculate.D2() </font>function to verify this is what happens when you pool that set of stats.</div><div><br></div><div>I do notice you have exogenous variables in your model, which lavaan treats as fixed by default (<font face=\"courier new, monospace\">fixed.x=TRUE</font>).  An older version of<font face=\"courier new, monospace\"> runMI() </font>set <font face=\"courier new, monospace\">fixed.x=FALSE</font> in all models, but I don&#39;t see how that would have changed your results very much. </div><div><br></div><div><div><div>Terrence D. Jorgensen</div><div>Assistant Professor, Methods and Statistics</div><div>Research Institute for Child Development and Education, the University of Amsterdam</div><div><a href=\"http://www.uva.nl/profile/t.d.jorgensen\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://www.uva.nl/profile/t.d.jorgensen&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792421835544000&amp;usg=AOvVaw0CsWSMiigx6U99Rj0nwH5C\">http://www.uva.nl/profile/t.d.jorgensen</a></div></div><div></div></div><div><br></div></div>"
    ]
